  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule in your local Kibana. The rule is enabled and its first run succeeded.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view has 14,074 web requests timestamped from 2026\u201110\u201104 to 2026\u201112\u201103. Each request records:\n- the client IP, the URL requested and its file extension\n- the response code, bytes sent, the visitor's OS and user agent\n- source and destination country, and a referrer\n\nResponse codes are stored as text: 12,832 are 200, 801 are 404 and 441 are 503. 503 is the only 5xx code in the data.\n\n**2. Dashboard: `EFF-5ddc1815 Web logs overview`** (id `f811f4e7-eb43-42d2-a8ee-272dd526215f`, default time range is the last 7 days). It has 8 panels:\n- Total requests, total bytes served, and unique visitors (counted by client IP), all built on the existing data view.\n- Error rate (4xx/5xx %): about 8.8% across all the data.\n- Requests over time, stacked by response code.\n- Top 10 requested URLs.\n- Top 10 source countries.\n- Average bytes served over time.\n\nThe dashboard saved without errors, and I ran each panel's query directly and they all return data. I haven't opened the dashboard in the browser to check how it looks.\n\n**3. Rule: `EFF-5ddc1815 5xx spike`** (id `c79ed536-d01e-4b73-8fc1-27985151def6`). It's an Elasticsearch query rule on the same data view:\n- **Filter:** `response.keyword >= 500 and response.keyword < 600`. I checked that this matches exactly the 441 documents with code 503.\n- **Fires when:** the count is more than 10 within the last 5 minutes.\n- **Schedule:** checks every minute.\n\nTwo things to know about the rule:\n- It has no notification actions attached, so a firing alert only shows up in Kibana's alerts view. Add a connector (email, Slack and so on) if you want to be notified.\n- Its first run found no active alerts. That's expected: most of the sample data is dated after today, so the last 5 minutes contain few or no 5xx responses.",
